---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import MotionStage from '@/components/motion/MotionStage.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import MotionOptions from '@/components/motion/MotionOptions.astro';
import MotionTokens from '@/components/motion/MotionTokens.astro';
import Text from '@/components/motion/scenes/Text.astro';
import { TextControls } from '@/components/motion/scenes/TextControls';
import { MotionTextReact } from '@/examples/react/motion-text.tsx';
import { MotionTextAngular } from '@/examples/angular/motion-text.ts';
import MotionTextSvelte from '@/examples/svelte/motion-text.svelte';
import { chipClass } from '@/components/motionDemo';
---
<MotionPage
  slug="text"
  title="Motion: animate text"
  description="Text split into words, characters or lines, each piece playing its keyframes in a cascade, with the text read once by screen readers. In HTML, React, Angular and Svelte."
>
  <MotionStage scene="text" label="Live example: Animate text" height={360}>
    <Fragment slot="controls"><TextControls client:visible size="page" /></Fragment>
    <Text size="page" />
    <Fragment slot="readout"><span class={`${chipClass} tracking-normal`} data-text-readout="all">split: 'words' · origin: 'first' · verb: 'reveal'</span></Fragment>
  </MotionStage>

  <h2>How to use it</h2>
  <p>
    A definition with <code>split</code> cuts the text of its element into pieces, and plays its keyframes on the pieces in a
    cascade, in the order of the text. <code>split</code> is <code>words</code>, <code>chars</code> (the letters, as
    graphemes) or <code>lines</code>, and it applies to <code>appear</code>, <code>disappear</code> and <code>reveal</code>.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`import { defineMotion } from '@udixio/motion';

defineMotion('headline', {
  verb: 'reveal',
  split: 'words',
  keyframes: [{ opacity: 0, y: 12 }, { opacity: 1, y: 0 }],
});`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Without a <code>stagger</code> the pieces cascade <code>tight</code>, and <code>origin</code> applies to them. In HTML the
    element names the motion as any other: <code>&lt;h2 data-motion="headline"&gt;…&lt;/h2&gt;</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <code>reveal</code> plays when the text enters the view. Text that is already in view when the page loads is content
    of the page, and keeps its natural state, as the rest of the page does: on a large screen the sentence above is there
    at once, and Replay plays it. Choose <code>appear</code> to play it as it is inserted.
  </p>

  <h2>How the text is kept</h2>
  <p>
    The original nodes move into a visually hidden copy, which keeps their semantics (<code>em</code>, <code>strong</code>,
    <code>abbr</code>). The cut copy that the eye sees is <code>aria-hidden="true"</code>, so a screen reader reads the text once,
    as written. A change of the text is cut again.
  </p>
  <p>
    An element that holds an interactive descendant (<code>a[href]</code>, <code>button</code>, <code>input</code>,
    <code>select</code>, <code>textarea</code>, <code>[tabindex]</code>, <code>[contenteditable]</code>) is not split: it plays
    whole, and a development warning says why. <code>lines</code> groups the words by their position, and a resize rebuilds the
    lines once the motion in progress has ended.
  </p>

  <h2>Use it in the frameworks</h2>
  <p>
    React <code>useMotion('headline')</code>, Angular <code>udxMotion="headline"</code>, Svelte
    <code>&#123;&#64;attach motion('headline')&#125;</code>. The examples reveal one sentence by words, and Replay plays it again.
  </p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-text">
    <MotionTextReact slot="react" client:visible />
    <MotionTextAngular slot="angular" client:visible />
    <MotionTextSvelte slot="svelte" client:visible />
  </MotionUsage>

  <h2>Options</h2>
  <p>The split is one of the options of a reveal, or of an appear. The page's example uses a reveal.</p>
  <MotionOptions verb="reveal" />

  <h2>Timing and tokens</h2>
  <p>The pieces move on the default spatial spring. Letters cascade tight, words normal and lines loose.</p>
  <MotionTokens tokens={['spring.default.spatial', 'stagger.tight', 'stagger.normal', 'stagger.loose']} />

  <h2>Reduced motion</h2>
  <p>
    Under reduced motion, or with <code>--udx-motion: none</code>, the text is not split: the element plays whole, with the
    reduced render of its verb. The definitions of the page use <code>reduced: 'fade'</code>, so the sentence fades in place.
  </p>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>lines</code> falls back to <code>words</code> when the text holds an element other than <code>br</code>.</li>
    <li>Joining scripts (Arabic, Indic, Thai and the like) are split by words in <code>chars</code>, so that no letter loses its form.</li>
    <li>A split element's children must not be added or removed by a framework: its text changes are followed, its elements are not.</li>
  </ul>
</MotionPage>
